<ContentLayout title="Aurora Security" description="Security principles, threat boundaries and verification status." eyebrow="ENGINEERING / SECURITY" heading="Security is structural." lead="Security claims must be scoped to a threat model, a concrete implementation and repeatable verification. No system is described as unbreakable.">
<section class="section shell article-content"><h2>Security review framework</h2><div class="principle-grid"><article class="principle-card"><span>01</span><h3>Threat model</h3><p>Identify assets, adversaries, entry points, trust assumptions and out-of-scope threats.</p></article><article class="principle-card"><span>02</span><h3>Privilege boundaries</h3><p>Document which component can access each resource and how that authority is granted or revoked.</p></article><article class="principle-card"><span>03</span><h3>Isolation</h3><p>Define failure containment and the security consequences of compromised services.</p></article><article class="principle-card"><span>04</span><h3>Verification</h3><p>Link tests and reports to explicit acceptance criteria; distinguish tests from independent audits.</p></article></div><h2>Minimum threat-model structure</h2>
<ul class="content-list">
  <li><strong>Assets:</strong> credentials, user files, cryptographic keys, system integrity, browser sessions and update artifacts.</li>
  <li><strong>Adversaries:</strong> malicious websites, untrusted extensions, compromised user-space services, malicious packages and attackers with physical access.</li>
  <li><strong>Entry points:</strong> boot media, device interfaces, IPC, file parsers, network protocols, browser rendering and extension interfaces.</li>
  <li><strong>Trust boundaries:</strong> kernel versus user space, service-to-service communication, browser privileged code versus web content, and verified versus untrusted packages.</li>
  <li><strong>Security objectives:</strong> least privilege, isolation, integrity, confidentiality, availability and recoverability—each with explicit scope and limitations.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Security claims require measurable evidence</h2>
<div class="principle-grid">
  <article class="principle-card"><span>01 / DESIGN</span><h3>Threat model</h3><p>Identify assets, adversaries, assumptions and out-of-scope threats before selecting controls.</p></article>
  <article class="principle-card"><span>02 / IMPLEMENTATION</span><h3>Enforcement</h3><p>Link each security requirement to the code path that enforces it and the failure modes it addresses.</p></article>
  <article class="principle-card"><span>03 / TESTING</span><h3>Adversarial validation</h3><p>Use negative tests, fuzzing and fault injection where appropriate; report coverage and residual risk.</p></article>
  <article class="principle-card"><span>04 / OPERATIONS</span><h3>Updates and response</h3><p>Document vulnerability intake, security updates, signing, rollback and incident communication.</p></article>
</div>
<h2>Current public status</h2><p class="content-copy">No current Aurora OS or Lumen security assessment has been reviewed for this website update. This page describes the publication standard, not a security certification or guarantee.</p><p><a class="text-link" href="/en/development/verification/">Verification reports ↗</a></p></section>
